<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1773 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Amazing Grace</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn written in 1772 by John Newton, a former slave-ship captain who changed his life, about being forgiven.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>John Newton</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (sung today to &quot;New Britain&quot;, a tune first published in 1829 in Columbian Harmony; earlier set to &quot;Hephzibah&quot; by John Jenkins Husband)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English evangelical Christians; later American churches, especially African American and shape-note singing communities</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Olney, Buckinghamshire, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written late 1772, probably first used 1 January 1773; first printed 1779 in Olney Hymns</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by John Newton (published 1779; he died in 1807))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>John Newton was born in Wapping, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1725 and went to sea at eleven. Pressed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, he deserted, was traded as crew to a slave ship and took up slave trading. For a time he was himself enslaved by the Sherbro people of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and forced to work on a plantation. In March 1748, aboard the Greyhound in the North Atlantic, a violent storm nearly sank the ship, and Newton called out to God for mercy. He marked this as his conversion, but it was not immediate: he went on in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> for several more voyages, now as a captain, buying people in African ports and carrying them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, holding the same view of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as his contemporaries. He stopped sailing after collapsing at about thirty.</p><p>Newton taught himself theology while working as a customs agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and was ordained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> in 1764 as curate of Olney, Buckinghamshire, a poor lace-making village. There he and the poet William Cowper wrote hymns for weekly prayer meetings. Newton wrote this one in late 1772 to illustrate a New Year's Day sermon, probably first used on 1 January 1773, on 1 Chronicles 17:16–17. It is not known whether it had any music; it may have been chanted. It appeared in 1779 in Olney Hymns, to which Newton gave 280 of 348 texts. After leaving Olney in the 1780s Newton became an outspoken abolitionist and worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, whose campaign led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade Act</a> 1807. Some scholars read the hymn's first stanza as Newton's reckoning with his part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, though Newton is not known to have connected the hymn to anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> views.</p><p>The hymn stayed obscure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> but spread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where four versions were printed between 1789 and 1799 and it was sung at the camp meetings of the Second Great Awakening. More than twenty tunes circulated until 1835, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> set the words to &quot;New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>&quot; in his shape-note tunebook Southern Harmony, which sold about 600,000 copies. Harriet Beecher Stowe's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uncle-toms-cabin">Uncle Tom's Cabin</a> (1852) has Tom sing it, with a verse not by Newton that had been passed down orally in African American communities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> sang a translation by Samuel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">Trail of Tears</a>, and it was printed in hymnals given to soldiers in the Civil War. Edwin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/othello">Othello</a> Excell's arrangement, published between 1900 and 1910, became the standard form in American churches.</p><p>Its first recording was by the Sacred Harp Choir in 1922. Mahalia Jackson's 1947 recording was widely broadcast, and during the civil rights movement she sang it for marchers; in 1964 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fannie-lou-hamer">Fannie Lou Hamer</a> led marchers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> singing it. Judy Collins recorded it in 1970 in opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, and it reached number 15 on the Billboard Hot 100; an instrumental by the Royal Scots Dragoon Guards spent five weeks at number one in the UK in 1972. Writers have called it an emblematic black spiritual, sung as an expression of deliverance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. It is sung at funerals and memorials, and in 2015 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barack-obama">Barack Obama</a> sang it at the memorial service for Clementa Pinckney, one of the nine people killed in the Charleston church shooting.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A first-person hymn of thanks: the singer looks back on a life of wrongdoing and despair and credits his rescue to God's undeserved mercy. It moves from confession to relief and then to trust that this mercy will carry him through future hardship and into eternity. It draws on the Gospel stories of the prodigal son and of a blind man healed.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Amazing grace! (how sweet the sound!)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That saved a wretch like me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I once was lost, but now am found,—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Was blind, but now I see.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Twas grace that taught my heart to fear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And grace my fears relieved;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>How precious did that grace appear</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The hour I first believed!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Through many dangers, toils, and snares.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I have already come;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Tis grace has brought me safe thus far,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And grace will lead me home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The Lord has promised good to me,—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His word my hope secures;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He will my shield and portion be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As long as life endures.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yes, when this flesh and heart shall fail,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And mortal life shall cease,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I shall possess, within the veil,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A life of joy and peace.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The earth shall soon dissolve like snow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The sun forbear to shine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But God, who call'd me here below,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Will be for ever mine.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: John Newton, Olney Hymns (1779). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Olney_Hymns_(1840)%2FBook_1%2FHymn_41" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="sUO7eDrZ8XE" data-title="Amazing Grace: St Andrew's Cathedral Choir, Sydney"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">St Andrew's Cathedral Choir, Sydney</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Music at St Andrew's Cathedral, Sydney. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sUO7eDrZ8XE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Uncle Tom's Cabin (Harriet Beecher Stowe)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1852</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cherokee translation by Samuel Worcester</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Whales &amp; Nightingales (Judy Collins)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Amazing Grace (Royal Scots Dragoon Guards, pipes and military band)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement based on Collins's recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sings Precious Memories (Johnny Cash)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1975</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Amazing Grace (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">referenced in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Freedom (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2014</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tells the story of the hymn's writing</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Newton's Grace (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2017</span><span class="sg-chain-h">referenced in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Camden 28 (documentary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2007</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version in the credits</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Amazing Grace (Bill Moyers documentary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">documentary about the song</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1 Chronicles 17:16–17</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the scripture the hymn was written on</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Parable of the Prodigal Son (Gospel of Luke)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the first verse's imagery</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Healing of the blind man (Gospel of John)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of a line</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jerusalem, My Happy Home (A Collection of Sacred Ballads)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1790</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the anonymous extra verse Stowe printed with the hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">New Britain (from &quot;Gallaher&quot; and &quot;St. Mary&quot;, Columbian Harmony)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1829</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune it is sung to since 1835</span></li></ul></section>
